{{smaller block|the greatest depths. In geology ''zone'' has nearly the same meaning as ''horizon''. A stratum, or a group of strata, may be characterized by the presence of a certain assemblage of fossils, or by one particular fossil; in such cases the most abundant or typical fossil may give a name to the subdivision in which it occurs, which will then be designated as the ''zone'' of that particular species. Thus, the Lower and the Middle Lias have together been divided into twelve zones, each characterized by the presence of a certain species of ammonite: as, the “zone of the ''Arietites'' (''Ammonites'') ''raricostatus'',” etc.}}
{{smaller block|They [the people of Savoy] would . . . lose their commercial ''zone'' or free frontier with Switzerland.
{{right|''C. K. Adams'', Democracy and Monarchy, ix.}}}}
{{smaller block|The ''zone'' of youthful fancy . . . is now well passed; the ''zone'' of cultured imagination is still beyond us.
{{right|''Stedman'', Vict. Poets, p. 15.}}}}
{{smaller block|How vast must have been that earlier period wherein were deposited those fine alternations of lime and clay which form hills, such as Mont Perrier, several hundred feet in height, divisible into distinct ''zones'', each characterised by peculiar assemblages of fossils.
